# A new office puts only its boot port, database and code folder in .env. It is never committed.
# Change running controls on Settings → Office controls; startup file settings are marked below. Saved choices live in the office database.
# The remaining declarations below are a reference for commands and older installations.
# A legacy environment or .env runtime value stays visible until a database choice replaces it.
#
# EVERY MOLE_OFFICE_ VARIABLE BELOW IS READ BY THE CODE, AND EVERY ONE THE CODE READS IS BELOW.
# tests/config.test.ts checks both directions against src/config/settings.mjs, which is where every
# setting is declared: its name, what it is for, how it is read and what it means when nobody sets
# it. The Settings page and this reference name each control — one written down nowhere
# is one nobody will ever turn, and one written down here that nothing reads is worse: it is a
# setting somebody will set, and then believe. `MOLE_OFFICE_MIRROR=./data/mirror` sat here doing
# exactly that, naming a feature this project does not have.
#
# RUNNING CONTROLS READ THE DATABASE FIRST. Without a saved choice, and for boot or command settings,
# the order is the environment this process was given, then this file, then
# the stated default. A setting that is present but EMPTY is refused rather than read as absent,
# except the two lists, where empty is a real instruction (watch nothing, preview nothing).
MOLE_OFFICE_PORT=8787

# The SQLite file everything is stored in. It is the only copy of your work.
#
# BACK IT UP WITH `npm run db:backup`, NOT BY COPYING THE FILE. This line used to say "one file;
# back it up by copying it", and that loses work: the database runs in write-ahead-log mode, so
# recent writes live in <file>-wal until a checkpoint folds them in. Measured against this
# project's own corpus on 2026-08-12, with the server running, `cp data/panelx.db backup.db`
# produced 423 records whose newest entry was 55 minutes old, while the database held 429. The
# backup command uses SQLite's online backup instead, so it is safe to run while the server is
# serving, and it verifies the copy and deletes it if it does not check out.
#
# STARTING A SERVER DOES NOT MIGRATE THIS FILE, and that is worth knowing before you set it to
# something you care about. It used to: the boot path ran every migration, `npm run dev` runs the
# server under `tsx watch`, and so a data migration that MOVES RECORDS BETWEEN PROJECTS could be —
# and on 11 August was — triggered by saving a source file, overnight, against the live corpus,
# with no backup taken at that moment.
#
# What a start does now depends on what is in the file, and it holds nothing anybody would miss in
# two cases: it has no records, links or changes at all — a fresh clone, a scratch file, the
# database a test builds — or every record in it is stamped `fixture`, which is what
# `npm run db:seed` leaves and what tools/review.mjs photographs. Either of those is migrated on the
# way up, because nothing in it can be lost.
#
# Otherwise the file is only ever READ at startup. If it is NOT up to date the server REFUSES TO
# SERVE, printing what a migration would write and the command to run: `npm run db:migrate`, which
# you run deliberately, after taking a copy. If it needs nothing, the server just starts.
#
# THE DEFAULT WAS ./data/panelx.db UNTIL 30 SEPTEMBER 2026. An office whose file is still there, with
# nothing naming another, is told so when it starts. Such an office keeps its file by naming it in its
# own .env:  MOLE_OFFICE_DB=./data/panelx.db
MOLE_OFFICE_DB=./data/mole-office.db

# Where `npm run dev` serves the browser app. Its own variable, because moving the app off 5173 —
# a port something else on this machine may be holding — has nothing to do with where the API is.
# Vite reads it to place the dev server. The API server reads it too, for the ONE thing it knows
# about the app: the two messages that send a lost caller to it — the 404 on a non-/api URL and the
# line printed at startup. Those used to say 5173 in hardcoded text, so moving the app here sent
# people to a dead address.
MOLE_OFFICE_WEB_PORT=5173

# Which checkout the source index scans. Defaults to the repository this server was started from,
# which is what you want unless you are indexing another tree. A path that is not a directory
# STOPS THE SCAN with a message naming this variable — it never falls back to the default, because
# indexing the wrong repository reports every claim in the real one as stale, and that reads as a
# codebase in trouble rather than as a typo here.
# MOLE_OFFICE_REPO_ROOT=/path/to/another-checkout

# Which PROJECT a command-line tool works in. Read by scripts/reindex.ts, which is the one script
# whose write lands in a workspace; over HTTP the same fact travels as the `x-mole-office-workspace`
# header. Unset means the default workspace — a stated default, not a guess, and the same one
# GET /api names — and an id that names no workspace is REFUSED rather than created, because a
# misspelt one would file the work into a project nobody has.
#
# PAIR IT WITH MOLE_OFFICE_REPO_ROOT DELIBERATELY. Each workspace holds its own source index, but one
# process still scans one checkout, so setting one of these without the other stores one project's
# file list under another project's name. Nothing can detect that — they are independent facts
# about this machine — so the reindex script prints both on its first line.
# Unset means `default`, the project every office has. An office that works in another project names
# it in its own .env, for example:
# MOLE_OFFICE_WORKSPACE=greenhouse

# WHO OWNS THIS OFFICE. Read once, when the database is first migrated, and then kept in the database
# (src/server/db/owner-setting.ts). Leave both out on a new office: it gives its owner the id `owner`,
# no name, and its pages lead the person to #/welcome, where they give their name themselves. Set them
# when you install an office for somebody who has already said their name. An office that already holds
# work and has no owner written refuses to start until both are set, and says so. After the first
# migrate the id must stay the same, and the name is not read again: it is the person's to change.
# MOLE_OFFICE_OWNER_ID=your-id
# MOLE_OFFICE_OWNER_NAME=Your Name

# Where the tools and tools/session-hook.mjs look for the API. The hook is the one thing here that
# Claude Code starts rather than this project's own commands, so there is no other way to tell it
# where to file a session.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED TO SET THIS WHEN YOU MOVE THE PORT. Unset means http://127.0.0.1: followed by
# MOLE_OFFICE_PORT, worked out in src/config/settings.mjs. This file used to say the opposite —
# "IF YOU MOVE THE API OFF 8787, SET THIS TOO. Nothing derives one from the other" — and on
# 28 August 2026 one office's port moved, nothing derived it, and the hook went on filing sessions
# into whatever answered on the old port: a scratch copy of the corpus with 791 records instead of
# the real 721. Set this only to point a tool at an API somewhere else.
# MOLE_OFFICE_API=http://192.0.2.10:8787
#
# tools/agent-worker.mjs reads it too, for the same reason: it is a process you start by hand,
# possibly on another machine, and it has to be told where Mole Office is.

# Which model a claude-code builder gets, and a claude-code run answering a message when
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHORE_MODEL is not set, when nothing on the Casting table, the Settings page or the
# agent's profile names one.
#
# THERE IS NO DEFAULT MODEL. Unset means no model: a run that gets
# none from the Casting table, the Settings page or the agent's profile is not started, and the
# refusal says where to name one. The usual place is the Casting page. The model a run was given is
# written onto the run, so a result never leaves you guessing which model produced it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_AGENT_MODEL=sonnet

# The runtime, model and reasoning effort for the runs the office starts by itself — answering a
# message in a group — apart from what builders get. Unset means what happens today: the agent's own
# registry row decides the runtime, the runtime is given the model it is given today, and no
# reasoning effort is passed. These move into the Settings store when U2 lands.
#
# opencode with a model reached through an OpenRouter key is the runtime these were added for. The
# reasoning effort is the provider's own word, for example max. A check does not take these: it
# starts claude with a fixed command line and reads MOLE_OFFICE_INSPECTION_MODEL.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHORE_RUNTIME=opencode
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHORE_MODEL=openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHORE_VARIANT=max

# The model and clock for a check of a finished run, when nothing on the Casting table or the checking
# agent's profile names a model. THERE IS NO DEFAULT MODEL: unset means no model, and a check that gets
# none is not started, with a refusal that points to the Casting page. Unset minutes means 40.
# These are separate from the model used for ordinary agent runs. The check records both choices.
# The inspector tool's --model and --limit flags override them for one check, including --dry.
# MOLE_OFFICE_INSPECTION_MODEL=sonnet
# MOLE_OFFICE_INSPECTION_MINUTES=40
# Seconds to wait for each complete inspection API response, separately from the model clock.
# Increasing this permits slower reads; it does not make the API faster. Unset means 60 seconds.
# MOLE_OFFICE_INSPECTION_API_SECONDS=60
# Only a request that never reached the office may be asked again.
# The office restarts for a few seconds when it is upgraded, or after a landing when it runs from
# the main checkout. Two minutes leaves room for a slow start without hiding an office that is
# really down.
# MOLE_OFFICE_INSPECTION_OFFICE_BACK_SECONDS=120
# How many checks the message worker may start in one lane in an hour. A further check waits its turn and says why;
# it is never refused. Unset means 6, and it may not be more than 6: that is the owner's cap, and raising it is the
# owner's decision. A value the office cannot use holds every check and names this setting.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHECKS_AN_HOUR=6

# The agent handles, separated by commas, whose runs tools/settle-owed-runs.mjs refuses to settle.
# Unset or empty means no agent is protected. The tool always refuses to settle the person's runs.
# MOLE_OFFICE_SETTLE_PROTECTED_HANDLES=some-agent,another-agent

# How many seconds tools/register-a-started-agent.mjs waits for Mole Office to answer before
# giving up. Unset means 60. It was a fixed 10 seconds, and that caused the problem this setting
# exists to fix: an agent could not register at exactly the moment several clusters were
# running and several agents were being started at once — which is the moment registration matters
# most, because that is when the office is busiest.
# MOLE_OFFICE_REGISTER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# The email address on a commit made by an agent in a sandbox, or by the merge queue. The name on
# the commit stays the agent's own handle, or "Mole Office merge queue". Unset means
# mole-office@example.invalid, an address that reaches nobody. An owner who wants commits under their
# own address sets it in their own .env. The launcher and the merge tool put it in GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL and
# GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL, so an agent's own git config does not change it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_COMMIT_EMAIL=you@example.com

# How many test workers the office's merge tool gives one suite run (npm test --maxWorkers), a whole number within the
# bounds declared with it in src/config/settings.mjs. Unset means 8. Measured on 23 and 24 September 2026, the whole
# suite took 31 minutes at 4, 17 at 8 and 12 at 12, and the few browser tests red under load pass when the queue runs
# them again alone. Use 12 only on a machine where nothing else runs tests.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MERGE_WORKERS=8

# The Modal VM test runner uses the two files below. Each has a default under ~/.cache/mole-office/modal.
# The token file. Unset means ~/.cache/mole-office/modal/token.toml. It must be a regular file owned by this user
# with mode 0600. Its contents are never copied to Modal.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MODAL_TOKEN_FILE=
# An executable Python with the modal package installed. Unset means ~/.cache/mole-office/modal/venv/bin/python.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MODAL_PYTHON=
# Before the merge queue bounces an entry for a test file red here alone, it runs that file once more on Modal,
# with the runner above. This is the most it may spend on that in one calendar month, in US dollars, counted from
# the runner's receipts. The shipped value is 42.5. Change it on Settings, Office controls: a changed budget
# needs the person's press or exact words. The amount must be non-negative, finite and whole cents.
# With the two default files in place and nothing set, the queue takes a second opinion and spends from this
# budget. When the token file is missing or is not a regular file of this user with mode 0600, or the Python is
# not an executable, the queue takes no second opinion and says which file, and whether its path came from the
# setting or the default.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MODAL_MONTHLY_CAP_USD=42.5

# The most tools/openrouter.mjs may spend through OpenRouter in one calendar month, in US dollars, counted from its
# receipts in ~/.cache/mole-office/openrouter. Unset or 0 means no cap of our own: the tool says so on each call, and
# still refuses a call the key's own limit at OpenRouter cannot cover. The owner has named no number.
# MOLE_OFFICE_OPENROUTER_MONTHLY_CAP_USD=0

# The merge queue's bus: one whole suite holds at most this many released entries of a lane,
# and leaves once it holds them or this many seconds after the first of them was released, whichever comes first.
# It leaves at once when no entry of the lane is left that could still be judged and board.
# The technical range is 1 to 64; the shipped cap is 8. Change it on Settings, Office controls, with the person's
# press or exact words. The technical upper bound limits replay and culprit-finding work in one batch.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BUS_MOST_ENTRIES=8
# MOLE_OFFICE_BUS_LEAVES_AFTER_SECONDS=1800

# The build sheriff: the agent, by handle or id, told about a test red on main alone, which belongs to no merge entry.
# Unset tells nobody; the office still files the problem and tells the secretary.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BUILD_SHERIFF=

# The agent handles, separated by commas, that `node tools/sandbox.mjs sizes --tell` warns when a sandbox folder
# passes 60 GB. Unset or empty tells nobody: the tool still lists the sizes and says which folder nobody was told
# about, a run that finds a folder passing 60 GB fails, and it warns on a later run once an agent is named here.
# MOLE_OFFICE_SANDBOX_DISK_WATCHERS=some-agent,another-agent

# The handle of the agent the scheduled timing job (tools/run-the-timing-job.mjs) addresses its result report to.
# Unset or empty files the report addressed to nobody, and the job then fails, naming this setting. A failed check
# still tells the secretary.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TIMING_REPORT_READER=some-agent

# ------- the message worker
#
# Since 4 September 2026 Mole Office starts a worker of its own when the API server starts. It reads
# the message queue, and for a message addressed to an agent it starts a run of that agent's tool
# with the message as the prompt. What it may start is limited: an agent tool, inside this checkout
# or a worktree of it, reaching the database only through the API, registered in the resources
# ledger and stoppable from the product.

# How many runs the worker may have going at once. Unset means two. Each run is a whole agent tool,
# so this is a limit on what one message can cost you in memory and in model calls at the same
# moment. A message that arrives while the limit is reached keeps its clock and its row says so.
# A whole number from 1 to 64; the Gates panel changes it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_WORKER_RUNS=2

# Start message delivery on this server. Set off for a preview of a copied corpus.
#
# Set merges-only to start the worker for the office's merge tool. The worker then starts
# tools/the-merger.mjs for the merge entries the office marks, and the sandbox launcher for the rounds
# asked through the next-round door. It starts no run for a message, so every message to an agent stays
# queued, as it does with off. It starts a check only while the office says checks run in that mode.
# Anything but on, off or merges-only is refused, and no worker setting is inferred from it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MESSAGE_WORKER=on

# Whether `mole-office start` starts the terminal holder: the small process that keeps the office's own
# terminals running while the server restarts. It is on unless this says off. With off, no holder is
# started and the office cannot open a terminal. Anything but on or off stops the command.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TERMINAL_HOLDER=on

# Whether the window starts when it is given a debugging switch, such as --remote-debugging-port or
# --inspect. It is off unless this says on. With off, the window refuses to start and says why, because
# a debugging switch lets any program on this computer read and press everything in the window. Turn it
# on only to debug the window. Anything but on or off stops the window.
# MOLE_OFFICE_WINDOW_DEBUGGING=off

# How large the server's V8 heap may grow, in whole megabytes from 2048 to 32768. tools/serve-dev.mjs
# passes it to the server it starts as --max-old-space-size, and the server's start line says the heap
# limit it got. Without it the server has Node's own limit. Anything else stops serve-dev and the server.
# MOLE_OFFICE_SERVER_HEAP_MB=12288

# Whether overlapping territories of running runs are refused (block), open a group (remind), or are not compared (off).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_TERRITORIES=remind

# Whether only plain house rules red may land: block, remind (file problems), or off (quiet).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_HOUSE_RULES=block

# Whether a few feature reds may land handed back: block, remind, or off.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_FEW_FEATURE_REDS=block
# Whether an entry red beyond a few feature files may land: block, remind, or off. A failed build never lands.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WIDE_RED=block
# Whether a test that changed its answer refuses the entry (block), files a problem (remind), or nothing (off).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_FLAKY=off
# Whether a merge entry whose tree with main does not build is sent back at the integrate step (block) or at its test step (off).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_INTEGRATED_BUILD=block
# Whether a merge entry that adds owner content to what ships is refused (block) or goes on with it (off). Only the person may change it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_OWNER_CONTENT=block
# Six guards of the merge queue's own steps. Each allows only block, so setting one to anything else is refused.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_THE_SEAL_MATCHES_THE_ENTRY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PINNED_CONTENT_IS_THE_SUBMISSION=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_FILES_ENTRY_BYTES_ARE_SEALED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_FILES_ENTRY_MATCHES_ITS_FILES_SEAL=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_LANDING_ONLY_ON_THE_TESTED_MAIN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_ONLY_WHAT_WAS_TESTED_LANDS=block
# Six guards of the merge tool's own steps. The first four allow only block. The last two allow block or remind, and stand at remind.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PINNED_COMMIT_IS_WHAT_WAS_SEALED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_RESOLUTION_REPLAYED_FROM_ITS_OWN_REF=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_TESTED_CLONE_IS_THE_INTEGRATED_RESULT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WITHDRAWN_ENTRY_TOOL_STOPS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PLUGIN_PAGES_BUILT_BEFORE_LANDING=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_OFFICE_HEALTHY_AFTER_LANDING=remind
# The most red feature files allowed; a whole number of at least 1.
# MOLE_OFFICE_FEW_RED_FILES=3
# Hours after landing to repair them; a whole number of at least 1.
# MOLE_OFFICE_HAND_BACK_HOURS=24

# Whether the merge queue may land a commit at the placeholder domain of MOLE_OFFICE_COMMIT_EMAIL's default:
# block, remind (the entry's record names each such commit), or off (only the merge tool's log names them).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PLACEHOLDER_ADDRESSES=block

# Before a run seals, remind it of what a check would most likely refuse; off seals at once without a reminder, and the separate hard migration check is never off.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_BEFORE_THE_SEAL=remind

# Before a run marked as method work or GO seals, remind it of a design that is missing, not reviewed, or short of what
# its project's own rules ask (remind), refuse the seal while the same check still finds one (block), or give no such
# reminder (off). Only the person changes it, on the Gates panel.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_REVIEWED_DESIGN=remind

# Refuse retirement while a check or a report is owed (block), or retire anyway (off).
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_RETIRE_WHILE_OWED=block

# Minutes that must pass after a stable release is completed before the next may begin; a whole number from 1 to 1440.
# The release tool reads it from the office's gate list. The number is a setting the person may change.
# MOLE_OFFICE_RELEASE_SPACING_MINUTES=180

# Whether one fix from main may go into the stable version before its tests, with an end time; 0 or 1.
# The release tool reads it from the office's gate list. The switch is a setting the person may change.
# MOLE_OFFICE_STABLE_EMERGENCY=0

# The shortest time, in minutes, between two firings of one counter; a whole number from 30 to 1440.
# A counter's creator may choose a longer time, never a shorter one. A firing that comes sooner is held back.
# MOLE_OFFICE_COUNTER_SPACING_MINUTES=30

# How many minutes an entry's own supervisor has to resolve a merge conflict; a whole number from 15 to 1440.
# When they end, the entry is sent back. An entry that is already resolving keeps the minutes it was given.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CONFLICT_RESOLVING_MINUTES=60

# How many times main may move while an entry is checked to land; a whole number from 1 to 20.
# When main moves once more, the entry is sent back.
# MOLE_OFFICE_LANDING_ATTEMPTS=3

# How many times the merge tool may run for an entry in one step without a result; a whole number from 1 to 20.
# After the last one, a landing entry stays and waits for a person to look, a resolving entry stays, and any
# other entry is sent back as stalled.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TOOL_RUNS_IN_ONE_PLACE=3

# The four kinds of standard change, released without an inspector after their own checks and the related tests pass:
# a change only inside one plugin, only to words or translations, only to tests, or only to documents. 1 or 0 each;
# at 0 a declaration of that kind goes the ordinary way, to the inspector.
# MOLE_OFFICE_STANDARD_ONE_PLUGIN=1
# MOLE_OFFICE_STANDARD_WORDS=1
# MOLE_OFFICE_STANDARD_TESTS=1
# MOLE_OFFICE_STANDARD_DOCUMENTS=1

# Who may act: one gate for each rule about who may do a thing, four for the holds on a message, and one for
# the hold on readiness signatures.
# Each is block or off, and block keeps the rule. Only the person may change one, or a secretary their grant
# lets press the settings door for them. Each rule is described on the Gates page.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_WRITES_A_RUNS_CONDITIONS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ENDS_A_RUN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_A_RUNS_PARENT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_FILES_A_SESSION=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_READS_A_HANDOVER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_RESUMES_A_RUN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_A_MESSAGE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_DELETES_A_MESSAGE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_A_FLOWS_ITEM=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_MAKES_A_CHECKLIST_FOR_THE_PERSON=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ACTS_ON_A_REVIEW=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SENDS_A_DRAFT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_FILES_OR_CLOSES_AN_INCIDENT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_HOLDS_A_NOTEBOOK=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SWEEPS_THE_LEDGER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_A_BOOKING=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ASKS_AND_ANSWERS_TO_GO_FIRST=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_REPORTS_ON_THE_GPU=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WAIT_FOR_THE_PERSONS_START=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_HELD_BY_AN_UNSENT_REVIEW=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_HELD_WHILE_A_RESIDENT_SLEEPS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_HELD_BY_A_NOTICE_RULE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_RELEASES_A_HELD_CHECK=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ASKS_FOR_A_CHECK_AGAIN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CONFIRMS_A_REPORT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CLOSES_A_RUN_AS_SUPERSEDED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_READS_A_SETTLEMENT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_THE_MERGE_QUEUE_OWNER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_PAUSES_THE_OFFICE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_THE_PAGE_WORDS_AND_SIDEBAR=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_THE_DEAD_LETTER_SECRETARY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_THE_MODULE_REMINDER_SECRETARY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_THE_MERGE_AND_CHECK_SWITCHES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_THE_CASTING_TABLE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_THE_GPU_WATCHER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_THE_OWNERS_SETTINGS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SWITCHES_WHETHER_AGENTS_MAY_INJECT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_THE_QUICK_INPUT_LIST=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHOOSES_THE_ADDRESS_FOR_BROWSERS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ENDS_ANOTHER_AGENTS_RAISE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_RETIRES_AN_AGENT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SAYS_WHETHER_ANOTHER_AGENT_READS_RELEASES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SETS_HOW_ANOTHER_AGENT_IS_ADDRESSED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_NAMES_A_MODULES_PLANNER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_REMOVES_AN_ASK_ON_THE_PERSON=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_RETIRES_A_ROW_THE_PERSON_STATED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_REPOINTS_A_SCANNED_REPOSITORY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CONFIRMS_A_PACKAGED_TASK_FINISHED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_ARRANGES_THE_MODULE_GRAPH=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SWITCHES_A_CHECKLIST_REMINDER=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_HELD_FOR_READINESS_SIGNATURES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_CHANGES_A_CARDS_WORDS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_REFUSES_A_ROLE_REQUEST=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_SUBMITS_A_MERGE_ENTRY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WHO_WITHDRAWS_A_MERGE_ENTRY=block

# Five checks the office's own server makes. Each is block or off, and block keeps the check. Only the person
# may change the first four; the person or a secretary may change the last. Each is described on the Gates page.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_GIVING_BACK_A_HELD_SEAT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WRITES_THAT_EMPTY_FIELDS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WRITES_FROM_A_STALE_COPY=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WRITES_OVER_UNSEEN_CHECKLIST_LINES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_WORKER_CUTS=block

# Six more checks of the office's own. Each allows only block, so setting one to anything else is refused.
# Nothing counts the third or the fifth, and their words on the Gates page say why.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_EVERY_SETTING_VALUE_IS_KNOWN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_DSH_CHECKS_TRANSCRIPT_IS_ITS_OWN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_OLD_SETTING_NAMES_STOP_THE_OFFICE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_AN_ACT_AT_AN_ASKING_DOOR_IS_PROVED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_TEST_WORLD_TRUSTS_ONLY_ITS_STARTER_AND_SECRETARIES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_THE_ROLE_CARDS_FILE_IS_USABLE=block

# Five checks of tools that run only on the host. Each allows one position, so setting one to anything else is
# refused. The third is not counted, and its words on the Gates page say why. The fourth only reminds.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_MODELS_LOGIN_OUTLASTS_THE_CHECK=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_CHECK_READS_ITS_OWN_WORKTREE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PROFILES_IN_USE_ARE_NEVER_WRITTEN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_START_REFUSAL_STOPS_THE_RETRIES=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_THE_OFFICE_STARTS_ONCE=block

# The sandbox tools' 18 checks. Each allows one position for now, the one its check acts by today, so setting
# one to anything else is refused. The first is the office's own; the sandbox tools make the other 17 and do not
# read their rows yet. Only the person may change them. Each is described on the Gates page.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_THE_OFFICE_VOUCHES_FOR_A_RUNS_SANDBOX=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_RAISE_HAS_A_REASON_AND_GOES_UP=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_CODEX_COMMAND_IS_CHECKED=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_OTHER_GPU_SANDBOXES_REMINDER=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_MAIN_REFRESHED_ONLY_FROM_ITS_OWN_CHECKOUT=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_SANDBOX_FOLDER_SIZE_WARNING=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_THE_HOSTS_RUN_LIST_IS_SOUND=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_ROUND_CONTINUES_ONLY_THIS_AGENTS_RUN=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_ROUNDS_RUN_WAS_FILED_HERE=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_ROUND_READS_ONLY_ITS_RUNS_OUTPUT=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_CODEX_RESUMES_ONLY_THIS_SANDBOXS_THREAD=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_OPENCODE_RESUMES_ONLY_THIS_SANDBOXS_SESSION=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_HANDSHAKE_ONLY_FOR_OWN_COMMANDS=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_RUN_IS_FILED_IN_ONE_SANDBOX=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_LAUNCH_LOGS_STAY_IN_THE_SANDBOX=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_PLAIN_ENV_STRIPS_THE_LAUNCHERS_VARIABLES=block
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_REFUSED_MESSAGE_IS_SAID_ANOTHER_WAY=remind
# MOLE_OFFICE_GATE_A_WORLD_COPY_IS_A_SOUND_DATABASE=block

# How far agents may answer each other before a person has to say something. Unset means three.
#
# An agent's reply can be a message to another agent, which starts another run, which replies again.
# Nothing in that loop needs a person, and nothing in it ends by itself. So the worker counts the
# chain back to the last message a person sent, and at this many hops it stops starting runs and
# writes on the row that the chain has gone far enough. It is a limit on a runaway, not a quota: a
# person answering anywhere in the chain sets the count back to nothing.
# A whole number from 0 to 100; the Gates panel changes it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_AGENT_HOPS=3

# How many messages one agent run may file through the worker. Unset means five.
#
# The limit above bounds how many runs are started, because a run is what costs a model call. It
# does not bound what one run writes. A run holds the API address and its own name, so a run that
# went wrong could file message after message into a group; every one of them is a stored row and
# every one announces itself to every open page, which makes the browser unusable without spending
# anything. So the worker's answered door counts the messages each run has filed, and past this many
# it refuses the next one with a sentence naming the limit and the run, and writes that sentence on
# the run's own session record.
# A whole number from 0 to 1000; the Gates panel changes it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_RUN_MESSAGES=5

# How many minutes a run that has answered waits for the next message. Unset means ten.
#
# A run answers and its process ends, but the conversation it made is still there and the tool can
# pick it up again by its own id. For this many minutes the next message to that agent goes into the
# same conversation, so the agent still knows what was said. When nothing comes, the conversation
# ends and a line in it says so; the message after that starts a fresh one.
# MOLE_OFFICE_RUN_WAITS_MINUTES=10

# How many Claude supervisor runs may go at once, across every project. Unset means six.
#
# A run counts while it runs in Claude Code, serves a supervisor, and has an open process row that
# has not ended. A run in any other program is not counted, dsh included, whatever model it uses.
# While this many go, the office starts no next round of a supervisor in Claude Code: the ask is
# kept, and the round starts when one of them ends. 0 starts none. A value that is not a whole
# number 0 or above is refused in words.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CLAUDE_SUPERVISORS_AT_ONCE=6

# When a Claude Code session the office starts compacts its conversation, in tokens: one setting for a
# conversation the message worker starts on this computer, and one for every run in a sandbox. Claude
# Code compacts at this number or at its model's limit, whichever is smaller, and takes 100000 to
# 1000000. A Casting row that names env.CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW in its settings uses its own
# number. Codex, OpenCode and dsh are given nothing. Both are changed on the Settings page, with no
# restart, and a change is used from the next start.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CLAUDE_COMPACTS_AT_ON_THE_HOST=500000
# MOLE_OFFICE_CLAUDE_COMPACTS_AT_IN_A_SANDBOX=200000

# How many hours an agent may go idle before the office reminds it of its role and duties. Unset
# means one hour.
#
# The idle flow: wait for work; past this many hours with nothing sent or received, the office
# sends the agent its role and duties once, with the note to re-read the office regulations if it
# has forgotten them (especially after a compaction); then it waits again and sends no further flow
# message until new work arrives — the heartbeat is untouched by this setting.
# MOLE_OFFICE_IDLE_REMINDER_HOURS=1

# The times of day a secretary broadcasts to the person, as HH:MM in local time separated by commas.
# Unset means 07:00,18:00.
#
# Every agent holding the secretary card reports to the person on a schedule of its own, and the office sends it a short reminder before each slot.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_TIMES=07:00,18:00

# How many minutes before a broadcast slot the office reminds the secretary. Unset means thirty.
#
# The reminder is one short line naming the slot, for example "18:00 the secretary's report".
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_LEAD_MINUTES=30

# How many broadcasts in a row may go unanswered by the person before the office pauses a secretary's
# schedule and tells the secretary once. Unset means three.
#
# Any answer from the person to the secretary starts the schedule again.
# A whole number from 1 to 100; the Gates panel changes it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_UNANSWERED_LIMIT=3

# The sentence the office adds to a secretary's LAST broadcast reminder of the day, so that once a day
# somebody checks that today's work has a second copy. Unset or empty means the last reminder says
# only its slot, like the others. Write the sentence your own office should read, for example:
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_BACKUP_REMINDER=Before today's last report, check that today's new code, data and results have a second copy.

# The id of the record that is the template for a secretary's report. Unset or empty means the
# reminders say nothing about a template, and no second reminder goes when a report has not come.
#
# When it is set, every broadcast reminder carries a button that opens the template, and the sentence
# that asks for a report on it at each broadcast time, with one chat line whose button opens the report.
# The id is a record of your own office, so this file names none.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_REPORT_TEMPLATE=

# How many minutes after a broadcast slot the office reminds a secretary once more, when a report
# template is set and the secretary has sent no report for that slot. Unset means fifteen.
#
# A report for the slot is a message from the secretary that carries the slot's mark
# (extra.broadcastSlot, the "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" the first reminder names) and a button (lead) that
# opens a report record. The office sends this reminder once per slot.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROADCAST_REPORT_LATE_MINUTES=15

# The one secretary reminded about each project's modules, as project=handle separated by commas, for
# example research=clerk,website=keeper. A module that is active, paused or has no state and has not
# changed in 14 days is said to that secretary in that project, at most once a day; stable and retired
# modules are not counted. One secretary per project, so a project with four secretaries does not get
# four lines a day saying one thing. Unset means no project gets a module reminder, and the server's
# log says so once for each project with modules left unchanged. A notebook needs no setting: one
# unchanged for 3 days reminds the agent it belongs to.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MODULE_REMINDER_SECRETARIES=

# The handle of the agent whose watcher reads the host GPU card, overriding the GPU watcher set on the
# Settings page. That agent and the person may hand in a reading of the card and record a cut-in step.
# Unset or empty means the one set on the Settings page counts, and nobody when none is set there.
# MOLE_OFFICE_GPU_WATCHER=some-agent

# Whether you are told, beside a GPU booking's holder, when a job with no booking leaves that booking no room on the
# card. 1 tells you once for each such job; 0 tells only the holder, and the Resources page still shows every job with
# no booking. It is changed on the Settings page, with no restart. Unset means 0.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TELL_THE_OWNER_OF_UNBOOKED_GPU_JOBS=0

# How often a session answering as a durable agent says it is still there. Unset means twenty seconds.
#
# A session that is open in a terminal holds its agent's handle, and `tools/hold-the-handle.mjs` says
# every this-many seconds that the session is still there. While it is saying so, a message to that
# agent goes to that session and Mole Office starts no run beside it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_HANDLE_BEAT_SECONDS=20

# THE MAIN WORLD'S REMINDER LIMITS. Many agents should not run
# in parallel in the main world (the host, outside any sandbox), and a few small jobs there are fine.
# When a supervisor's or worker's run on the host goes past its limit, the office tells whoever started
# it, once, to use a sandbox. It never stops or refuses anything. A run in a sandbox is never counted.
#
# x and a number is so many per secretary: each secretary's own tree of starters has that share, and the
# office's total is that many times the live secretaries. A plain number is the total for the whole
# office. Unset means one supervisor and two workers per secretary.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MAIN_WORLD_SUPERVISORS=x1
# MOLE_OFFICE_MAIN_WORLD_WORKERS=x2

# How long a session answering as a durable agent may go quiet. Unset means ninety seconds.
#
# Past this, the hold is released with that reason by the next reader and the worker goes back to
# starting runs. NOTHING IS SIGNALLED: the session may still be alive and busy, and this says only
# that Mole Office stops sending it messages. It must be longer than the beat above, and a shorter
# one is refused naming both settings rather than quietly corrected — every hold would otherwise
# lapse the moment after it was written and no live session would ever answer.
# MOLE_OFFICE_HANDLE_LAPSES_AFTER_SECONDS=90

# How long a handle stays its session after that session stops saying it is there. Unset means two
# minutes.
#
# The waiter for a Claude Code session EXITS in order to wake it, so the beat stops every time a
# message arrives and starts again once the session has answered. Past the lapse above the handle
# stops reading as answering; past this window as well it is given up. Measured on 7 September 2026:
# the gap was ninety-two seconds against a ninety-second lapse, and a run took the handle and wrote
# in a group as the secretary while the secretary was writing something else. It must be longer than the lapse, and a shorter one is refused
# naming both settings rather than quietly corrected.
# MOLE_OFFICE_HANDLE_GRACE_SECONDS=120

# How long a message handed to the session holding a handle may stay unread. Unset means ten minutes.
#
# The hold's beat comes from the waiter's own process, so a fresh beat does not say the session reads.
# When a message was handed over and the session's transcript has not grown since, for longer than
# this, the handle says it is held but nobody has read since the transcript last grew. From then a
# message to the agent also goes up its chain of starters, as when nothing answers.
# MOLE_OFFICE_HANDLE_UNREAD_AFTER_SECONDS=600

# How many minutes a person's message handed to an agent's live session may go without a reply from
# that agent in the same conversation before Mole Office reminds the agent, once for that message.
# A reply is a message from the agent to that person in the same group, or in the direct conversation
# when the message went to no group. Unset means three minutes.
# MOLE_OFFICE_REPLY_REMINDER_MINUTES=3

# How long after a message is written its writer may still change its words. Unset means two minutes.
#
# Only the writer may change a message's words (its title and body), and only inside this window. The
# first change keeps the words as first written beside the new ones,
# so a reader can see the message was edited and what it said before. After the window the words are
# fixed, and a change is refused with a sentence saying why.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MESSAGE_EDIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=120

# Which durable agent a session started in this checkout answers as. Unset means none.
#
# A terminal knows its own conversation id and nothing else, so this is the one place that says which
# Mole Office agent its sessions are. When it is set, the session hook files this session's record
# under that agent and offers to start the waiter; when it is not set, a session here is nobody's
# durable agent and the hook offers nothing, which is what every checkout got before this existed.
# MOLE_OFFICE_AGENT=your-agent-handle

# A script that answers every message INSTEAD OF AN AGENT TOOL. For the test suite, and nothing else.
#
# The test suite has to exercise the whole path — a message queued, a run started, an answer read, a
# reply filed, a mark moved — and doing that with a real agent tool would spend a model call per
# test. Setting this to tests/fake-runtime.mjs answers every message with a script instead. While it
# is set, the runtime page and the worker's own health row say in words that runs are being faked,
# because a reply that never came from a model must never be presented as one. Unset is off, and off
# is what an installation is meant to be.
# MOLE_OFFICE_FAKE_RUNTIME=tests/fake-runtime.mjs

# The word that lets `npm run db:reset` delete a database that HOLDS RECORDS.
#
# That command deletes a database and seeds a fresh corpus in its place, and its default target is
# the same path the server uses — one word away from `npm run db:migrate` in the same package.json,
# on a project whose only copy of its own research record lives there. It now refuses a database
# holding records, names the count, and offers the two ways on. This is the second of them.
#
# Unset, or anything other than 1, means refuse. An absent or empty database is what the reset is
# FOR, so that case is never blocked and never warns — the test suite and the review tool build
# throwaway databases constantly and must stay quiet.
#
#   MOLE_OFFICE_DB=/tmp/scratch.db npm run db:reset          reset a throwaway one instead
#   MOLE_OFFICE_RESET_YES_DELETE_MY_DATA=1 npm run db:reset   really delete the one MOLE_OFFICE_DB names
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_RESET_YES_DELETE_MY_DATA=1
#
# Before it deletes a file, the reset also asks the office this checkout names whether a live world
# holds it, and refuses if one does. When nothing listens at that address, the ledger cannot be
# asked, and the reset refuses unless this is 1. It passes only a refused connection: an office
# that does not answer in time may still hold the database, and no word passes that.
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_RESET_WITHOUT_THE_LEDGER=1

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- other computers
#
# THE SECOND LISTENER, AND UNSET MEANS OFF. Absent is the state every installation of Mole Office is in
# and it is not a mistake: nobody who has not set this has done anything wrong. There is no default
# port, because turning this on is naming a number and a default would be Mole Office deciding to listen
# for other machines because nobody said not to.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. Mole Office has no login, so MOLE_OFFICE_PORT above is bound to 127.0.0.1 and
# nothing but this computer can open it. Setting this opens a SECOND listener, also on 127.0.0.1,
# for a private network daemon to pass your other machines through to — Tailscale Serve is what this
# was designed against. The bind address never changes. Nothing on your house network, your office
# network or a container bridge can reach either listener, before or after you set this.
#
# WHAT CHANGES WHEN YOU DO. A request that arrives on the second listener is stamped as coming from
# another machine, and every act Mole Office keeps here is refused to it: anything that touches this
# computer's files, ports or processes, anything that would start work here, and the handful of acts
# Mole Office reserves to you. GET /api/access lists exactly which, and the Settings page draws it.
# BEING SOMEWHERE ELSE IS A PERMISSION AND NOT A DEMOTION — an ordinary write from another machine
# goes through and is still recorded as yours, because it is yours.
#
# SET AND BLANK IS REFUSED rather than read as absence, exactly as MOLE_OFFICE_PORT is. An unset variable
# in a template produces a blank one, and reading that as "off" would be guessing.
#
# TWO THINGS THIS DOES NOT DO. It does not put Mole Office on the internet: something still has to
# be told to pass requests to this port, and that is a command you run outside Mole Office. And it
# does not give Mole Office a login — it cannot tell you from anything else on your own private
# network, which is why the acts above are refused rather than asked about.
#
# RUNNING THE BUILT APP IS PART OF IT. `npm run dev` serves the browser app from a second process
# that is not exposed, so another machine reaching this port gets the API and no app. Run the built
# app — `npm run build` then `NODE_ENV=production npm start` — to let another machine open
# Mole Office.
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_REMOTE_PORT=8788

# THE OTHER WAY, AND IT NEEDS NO DAEMON. Set this to the one name or address your other machines
# reach this computer at — a Tailscale name or address is what it was built against — and Mole Office
# listens on it as well as on 127.0.0.1. Both the API and `npm run dev` do. Nothing is passed
# through by anything: the address is bound directly, so what can reach it is exactly what can reach
# that address.
#
# UNSET MEANS OFF and unset is the state every installation is in. There is no default, because a
# default here would be Mole Office deciding to listen for other machines because nobody said not to.
# A scratch stack — a test, a worktree, a throwaway database — stays on 127.0.0.1 unless its own
# environment names an address.
#
# IT IS A NAME OR A NUMBER, NEVER A WHOLE ADDRESS. The ports are MOLE_OFFICE_PORT and
# MOLE_OFFICE_WEB_PORT above; writing one here as well would be two places that can disagree. So
# `192.0.2.10` and `my-computer.example` are right, and `http://…:5173` is
# refused. An IPv6 address goes in square brackets.
#
# `0.0.0.0`, `::`, `*` AND `0` ARE REFUSED BY NAME. They mean every address this computer has.
# Mole Office has no login, so binding all of them would put this research diary on your house
# network and on any container bridge, and nothing on screen would say it had happened.
#
# `none` MEANS OFF, and it is how a scratch stack turns this off again. Once this line is here,
# every process started from this checkout inherits it, because an environment can override a value
# in this file but cannot take one away. A test, a worktree or a throwaway server that must stay on
# 127.0.0.1 puts MOLE_OFFICE_ADDRESS_FOR_OTHER_MACHINES=none in its own environment. An empty value
# is still refused: `none` is somebody saying no, and a blank is nobody saying anything.
#
# WHAT A MACHINE THAT REACHES IT MAY DO is the same as for the second listener above: the request is
# stamped as coming from another machine, and every act Mole Office keeps here is refused to it.
# GET /api/access lists exactly which, and the Settings page draws it.
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_ADDRESS_FOR_OTHER_MACHINES=192.0.2.10

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the visitor card ceiling
#
# THE HIGHEST ROLE CARD A VISITOR MAY HOLD. By default a visitor holds at most the supervisor card,
# never the inspector or the secretary card, and the person may change that here.
#
# A VISITOR IS AN AGENT THAT CAME IN THROUGH THE EMBASSY ON A CREDENTIAL. The card its credential asks
# for, and the card a confirmation gives, is refused above this ceiling with a sentence naming the
# ceiling and this setting.
#
# UNSET MEANS SUPERVISOR. The inspector and the secretary are never given to a visitor by the
# default, because the person appoints both with their own press. Setting this to a card name raises the
# ceiling, and the row then says the ceiling was raised by this setting.
# MOLE_OFFICE_VISITOR_CARD_CEILING=supervisor

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- session thresholds
#
# How long a session may be quiet before Mole Office changes the word it uses for it. Both values
# below are the built-in defaults, so leaving them out changes nothing; they are written here
# because they are judgements, and a judgement nobody can find is one nobody can argue with.
#
# Format: a whole number and one of s, m, h, d — 30s, 5m, 2h, 1d. A bare number is refused
# rather than read as seconds, and any malformed value STOPS THE SERVER AT STARTUP naming the
# variable. It never falls back to the default: a threshold that quietly reverted would relabel
# every session on the page, and nothing about the page would look wrong. The effective values
# are logged once on every start for the same reason.
#
# Quiet this long, with a live process holding the session, and it reads as `idle` — alive, but
# most likely waiting on you.
# MOLE_OFFICE_IDLE_AFTER=5m
#
# Quiet this long, with NO process holding it, and it reads as `orphaned` — nobody is watching
# it and it never said it had finished. This was 6h. It is 2h because an orphan holding a GPU, a
# port or a database file for six hours is six hours of a contended resource that nobody knew was
# gone. Must be longer than MOLE_OFFICE_IDLE_AFTER. See explore.md.
# MOLE_OFFICE_ORPHAN_AFTER=2h
#
# When is picking a run up again expensive? Mole Office warns on a run whose last turn was large
# and which has been idle a while, because resuming it re-reads a long conversation. These two
# values are the two halves of that judgement and are separate from the session words above.
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_EXPENSIVE_AFTER is how long a run may be quiet before picking it up is called
# expensive. The default, 5m, is the provider's own prompt-cache window: after five minutes of
# quiet the cache is gone and resuming costs much more. Format is the same duration format as the
# thresholds above.
# MOLE_OFFICE_EXPENSIVE_AFTER=5m
#
# MOLE_OFFICE_LARGE_LAST_TURN_TOKENS is how many input tokens a run's last turn must report before
# it counts as large. The default, 100000, is half the 200000-token context window the model
# reported for itself. A missing receipt is not zero: a run nobody measured carries no warning.
# MOLE_OFFICE_LARGE_LAST_TURN_TOKENS=100000

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the lock ledger
#
# Where the resource ledger looks for lock files, colon-separated like PATH. Absolute paths only:
# a relative path would mean a different directory depending on where the server was started
# from. The default is the line below; set it to an empty string to watch nothing, which is
# honoured rather than overridden.
#
# This list is also a BOUNDARY, not only a search list. A session may register a lock file only
# if it sits under one of these paths, because the ledger is written to by agents: without a
# boundary, one agent with a wrong path in its own configuration could point Mole Office at any
# directory on the disk and have it watched and reported.
#
# Mole Office NEVER DELETES A LOCK FILE — the release endpoint refuses locks outright, with no
# override. A lock is often the only evidence that a job is still running, and removing it lets a
# second copy start on the same data, which is worse than the stale lock and silent besides. The
# report tells you where the file is and you remove it yourself.
# MOLE_OFFICE_LOCK_PATHS=/tmp:/var/lock

# How many browser test files may run at once on this machine. Unset means four, the number of
# slot directories the hand-made scheme of 11 September used. The office hands the seats out through
# POST /api/slots/acquire and node tools/slot.mjs; a whole test suite has a pool of its own, set below.
# Every browser shares the machine's inotify instances, so raise this only after measuring. A whole
# number from 1 to 64; the Gates panel changes it.
# MOLE_OFFICE_BROWSER_TEST_SLOTS=4

# How many whole test suites may run at once on this machine. Unset means one. Two at once stopped the
# person's server on 11 September 2026. A whole number from 1 to 64; only the person changes it, on the
# Gates panel.
# MOLE_OFFICE_WHOLE_SUITE_SLOTS=1

# How many batches of named test files may run at once on this machine. Unset means three. Each run of
# node tools/run-named-tests.mjs asks for a seat before its first file and gives it back after its last.
# A whole number from 1 to 64; only the person changes it, on the Gates panel.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TEST_BATCH_SLOTS=3

# The one-minute load average above which no new test batch is given a seat. Unset means 14. A batch
# already running keeps its seat. A whole number from 1 to 1000; only the person changes it, on the Gates
# panel.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TEST_BATCH_LOAD_LIMIT=14

# The gigabytes free on the root file system below which no new test batch is given a seat. Unset means
# 90. A gigabyte is 1,000,000,000 bytes, as df -B1 counts. A batch already running keeps its seat. A
# whole number from 1 to 100000; only the person changes it, on the Gates panel.
# MOLE_OFFICE_TEST_BATCH_FREE_GB_LIMIT=90

# Which directories inline preview may serve a file out of, colon-separated.
#
# THE VARIABLE HAS THREE STATES AND THEY ARE NOT TWO. This said "empty means none: with nothing set,
# every preview is refused", which was true of the line below and false about the code — and a
# sentence that is wrong about where a server may read bytes from is worse than no sentence.
#
#   MOLE_OFFICE_PREVIEW_ROOTS=            set and empty  -> NO roots. Every preview is refused.
#                                                      This is what the line below does, and what a
#                                                      fresh clone copying this file gets.
#   (the line deleted entirely)      not set at all -> the two directories Mole Office writes into
#                                                      itself: the checkout, and ~/.cache/mole-office
#                                                      when that directory is there. Not "nothing".
#                                                      Absent and empty are different states here,
#                                                      deliberately — see files.ts.
#   MOLE_OFFICE_PREVIEW_ROOTS=/a:/b       set            -> exactly those, each absolute, each resolved,
#                                                      AND the directories derived for the project.
#
# AND THE DERIVED DIRECTORIES ARE IN FORCE IN EVERY STATE EXCEPT "PREVIEW NOTHING". They are the
# checkout, ~/.cache/mole-office, the root of every repository registered in the project, and every
# existing directory a registered work area of the project names under one of those roots. They are
# added BESIDE this variable and never in place of it, so nothing here narrows when a repository is
# registered: the roots in force are the union. To reach a picture under a registered repository,
# register the repository in Settings, then the Repositories panel; do not ask the person to edit
# this file for it. An empty value still means "preview nothing", the derived directories included.
#
# A path being listed here is NOT on its own enough to serve a file — a record must already point at
# it. The root list is the outer fence; the reference is the inner one, and a request has to pass
# both. See src/server/api/files.ts, which states the whole confinement contract.
#
# To preview an image that lives outside this checkout and outside every registered repository — the
# research archive, say — name its directory here on purpose. That is an operator decision and Mole
# Office will not make it for you.
# STARTUP FILE SETTINGS: preview roots
# MOLE_OFFICE_PREVIEW_ROOTS is set in this startup .env file.
# It is shown read-only on Settings and is not an Office controls press.
# MOLE_OFFICE_PREVIEW_ROOTS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- avatars
#
# THE LARGEST PICTURE SOMEBODY MAY UPLOAD AS THEIR AVATAR, in bytes. Two megabytes by default.
#
# An avatar is drawn small, in a list of contacts and beside a name, so a picture bigger than this is
# almost always a photograph nobody meant to send. The refusal says both numbers — how big the file
# was and what the limit is — so anybody who meets it knows by how much.
#
# Uploaded pictures are stored under the directory holding your database, in `avatars/`. They are
# never in this repository: a test fails if anything under `data/` is tracked by git, and so does
# `node tools/open-source-gate.mjs`.
# MOLE_OFFICE_AVATAR_MAX_BYTES=2097152

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- a picture in a chat message
#
# THE LARGEST PICTURE THAT MAY BE SENT IN A MESSAGE, in bytes. Ten megabytes by default.
#
# It is five times the avatar limit because it is a different thing: an avatar is drawn small beside
# a name, and this is a screenshot of somebody's whole screen. It stays well under the 25 MB inline
# preview cap, so a picture that was accepted can always be drawn back.
#
# The composer says the limit and the three file types before it refuses anything, so nobody meets
# this number for the first time after choosing a file.
#
# A picture sent this way is stored beside your database, in `message-pictures/`, the way an avatar
# is. It is not in your database: the message record points at the file, exactly as a report points
# at a screenshot, and the bytes come back through the same `GET /api/file` door. That door reads
# only inside MOLE_OFFICE_PREVIEW_ROOTS, so an upload is REFUSED — before anything is written — when
# the database sits somewhere no preview root reaches. The refusal names the directory and the roots.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MESSAGE_PICTURE_MAX_BYTES=10485760

# ------------------------------------------------------- a file brought back from outside
#
# THE LARGEST FILE SOMEBODY MAY BRING BACK FROM OUTSIDE, in bytes. Fifty megabytes by default.
#
# A zip comes in through one new door built the way the pictures
# door is, stored beside the pictures, never opened by the office, refused above 50 MB, and kept as
# long as the record that points at it. A returned file is bigger than a chat picture on purpose:
# it carries a whole piece of outside work rather than one screenshot.
#
# The read at GET /api/returned-files says this number and the one type before anybody chooses a
# file, so nobody meets the limit for the first time after choosing. A returned file is stored
# beside your database, in `returned-files/`, the way a picture sent in a message is stored in
# `message-pictures/`. It is never in your database, and the office never opens it: only the first
# four bytes are read, to see that it really is a zip. Nothing prunes these files, so a file whose
# record is deleted stays on disk.
# MOLE_OFFICE_RETURNED_FILE_MAX_BYTES=52428800

# THE LARGEST A PERMANENT RESIDENT'S PERSONAL SPACE MAY GROW, in bytes. 500 MB by default.
#
# A resident's personal space holds at most 500 MB by default, and the limit is a setting. A space is a folder beside your database, in `personal-spaces/<agent id>/`, reached
# only through the office's doors. A write that would pass the limit is refused with a sentence
# naming the size used and the limit; nothing in a space is ever cut or deleted. Every backup
# carries the spaces whole, outside the 10 GB line on new bytes for the other files beside the database.
# MOLE_OFFICE_PERSONAL_SPACE_MAX_BYTES=524288000

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- old code
#
# HOW LONG OLD CODE HAS TO STAY OLD BEFORE THE PAGE SAYS SO, in seconds. Fifteen by default.
#
# The page draws a red banner when the code it is running, or the code the server it talks to is
# running, is older than the checkout. Both are normal for a second or two while a save is being
# picked up: a `tsx watch` restart takes about two seconds, and Vite hands the page new code a
# moment after that. A banner in that window would be the product shouting about its own reload.
#
# It is also what stops a false banner during the test suite. tests/freshness-is-provable.test.ts
# touches src/shared/time.ts on purpose, to prove a watcher picks it up, and puts the modification
# time back at the end. Without this grace that made every browser page served earlier in the same
# run read as older than the checkout, and three browser tests came up behind a red banner that was
# telling the truth about a file nobody had really changed.
#
# Raise it if your machine is slow to reload; lower it to nothing and every save flashes a banner.
# MOLE_OFFICE_STALE_CODE_GRACE=15

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- the dev supervisor
#
# HOW OFTEN THE DEV SUPERVISOR CHECKS FOR A CHANGE ITS WATCHES MISSED, in seconds.
#
# `npm run dev:server` watches the folders the server's code comes out of and restarts it when one
# of those files changes. Every minute it also does a slow check: it reads the newest modification
# time of the files the running server was built from and compares it with the moment that server
# was started. A file newer than the running server, with no restart in between, is a change nobody
# saw, and the restart it causes says so.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE: on 4 September 2026 the API on this machine served pre-merge code with health
# saying ok false and no restart landing. Raise it to check less often; each check costs one walk
# of the file list.
# MOLE_OFFICE_CHECK_FOR_MISSED_CHANGES=60

# HOW MANY FAILED STARTS IN A ROW BEFORE THE SUPERVISOR WRITES IT WHERE THE PRODUCT CAN READ IT.
#
# The supervisor restarts a server that stopped on its own, waiting longer each time up to half a
# minute. On 4 September 2026 it did that ninety-six times in a row, every one of them failing, for
# forty-seven minutes, and the only trace was a line in a log file nobody had open. After this many
# failures it writes `.logs/the-supervisor-cannot-start-a-server.json`, and the runtime record,
# `GET /api/health` and the page all read that file and name the failure.
# MOLE_OFFICE_FAILED_STARTS_BEFORE_SAYING=3

# HOW LONG A SERVER THE SUPERVISOR HAS JUST STARTED HAS TO ANSWER, in seconds.
#
# The supervisor holds the office's port and hands it to each server it starts. A server that stays
# alive but never answers would leave every request waiting on that port with no reply. After this
# long the supervisor stops that server by its process group, answers on the port with 503 and the
# reason, and starts the server again as it does after a crash. The time the server spends on its
# migrations is not counted. After three servers in a row are stopped this way, it starts no new one
# until a file the server is built from changes. The start guard has taken 14.9 seconds before now,
# so keep this well above that. A whole number from 1 to 86400. It is a row of the Gates page.
# MOLE_OFFICE_A_NEW_SERVER_HAS_TO_ANSWER_WITHIN=60

# HOW LONG A SERVER THE SUPERVISOR HAS STARTED MAY RUN ITS MIGRATIONS, in seconds.
#
# While a server runs its migrations, the supervisor answers every request on the port with 503
# and says the office is migrating. Past this long it stops that server by its process group and
# starts no new one until a file the server is built from changes, because a new one would run the
# same migrations. A start that finds a database holding real work only checks it and measures
# the migrations on a copy: on a 2.16 GB copy of the live database that took 17 seconds warm, and
# the integrity check alone 43 seconds cold. The longest wait the start itself allows is 10 minutes.
# A whole number from 1 to 86400. It is a row of the Gates page.
# MOLE_OFFICE_MIGRATIONS_HAVE_TO_FINISH_WITHIN=900

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- stray servers
#
# HOW LONG A SERVER MAY BE RUNNING BEFORE NOBODY WAITING FOR IT IS A PROBLEM, in seconds.
# `node tools/stray-servers.mjs` lists Mole Office servers on this machine that no session claims; a
# server younger than this is simply starting up and is not reported. Two minutes by default.
# MOLE_OFFICE_STRAY_GRACE=120

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------- the test suite's Vite
#
# START NO FILE WATCHER. Set to 1 by tests/helpers.ts and by nothing else; you never set this.
#
# It is written down here because the rule for this file is that every MOLE_OFFICE_ variable the
# code reads is findable in it, and vite.config.ts reads this one.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: a watching Vite holds one inotify instance, Linux allows a user 128 of them on the
# whole machine, and the suite starts fifty-two of these. When they ran out the watcher died with
# EMFILE and took the test file with it. Nothing edits a source file during a test, so those
# watchers were waiting for a change that never comes. The measurement is in vitest.config.ts.
#
# Unset means watch, which is what `npm run dev` gets. Any value other than 1 stops Vite rather than
# being read as one of the two.
# MOLE_OFFICE_NO_WATCH=1
#
# WHERE VITE KEEPS ITS PRE-BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES. Set by tests/helpers.ts, to a directory inside the
# throwaway one each browser test already makes and deletes. You never set this either.
#
# Unset means `.cache/vite` inside the checkout the config file is in — one cache per checkout,
# which is the point. Vite's own default is node_modules/.vite, and a worktree's node_modules is a
# symlink to the main checkout's, so every checkout on the machine used to write into one cache
# while the owner's dev server was reading it. vite.config.ts has the whole account.
#
# Set and empty is refused rather than read as unset. A relative path is taken from the checkout.
# MOLE_OFFICE_VITE_CACHE=/tmp/my-vite-cache

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- nothing dead
#
# There is no "not read yet" section any more, and that is deliberate.
#
# This file used to carry three OPENROUTER_ variables under a heading saying nothing read them.
# They were the planned integration for generated summaries and question search. On 19 August 2026
# the owner revoked the key and settled the question: Mole Office is not getting provider, model or
# credential settings in this round, and a dead credential under a name the product does not read
# is what costs somebody an afternoon in six months. So they are gone rather than commented out.
#
# The rule that replaces the old heading: every variable named in this file is read by the code.
# tests/config.test.ts holds both directions of that, and there is no longer a place to park one
# that is not.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the demo corpus
#
# THE CORPUS `npm run db:seed` WRITES, as a JSON file. Unset means the public one in this
# repository, `scripts/seed-corpus/public.json`: an invented project, so that a fresh clone seeds
# something that describes nobody.
#
# The seed is a program that builds records, links, pipeline steps and a fabricated history from a
# corpus file. The file holds the words; the program holds the shape. Point this at a corpus of your
# own and `npm run db:seed` writes that instead, through exactly the same checks.
#
# A file that is not there, or that is not the shape the seed reads, STOPS THE SEED with a message
# naming this variable and the path. It never falls back to the public corpus: seeding somebody
# else's demo into a database you meant to fill with your own is not something you would notice.
# MOLE_OFFICE_SEED_CORPUS=/path/to/your-corpus.json

# HOW MANY RECENT EVENTS THE SERVER REMEMBERS, so a page that reconnects after a break can be told
# what it missed. A page that was away longer than this is told the number it cannot have, and reads
# the pool again rather than believing it saw everything.
# MOLE_OFFICE_EVENT_RING=200

# HOW LONG SOMEBODY MAY BE QUIET BEFORE THEY STOP READING AS ONLINE. The first is for a durable
# agent, counted from the last thing that arrived from it. The second is for a person, counted from
# the last time their messages panel said it was open.
# MOLE_OFFICE_ALIVE_WITHIN_MINUTES=5
# MOLE_OFFICE_PERSON_ALIVE_WITHIN_SECONDS=60

# THE MESSAGE DOOR'S OFFLINE REFUSAL. An agent sender is
# refused a recipient that is retired or not registered either way. This one adds the wider refusal:
# a recipient nothing is answering as — no session holds its handle, no run of it is going, no
# connection is open. It defaults to OFF on purpose: presence is not yet true for an agent started as
# a subagent (every worker of this office reads grey while working, because only a session running a
# waiter reads green), and until M106 (Cluster B) and F9 make presence true for them, refusing on
# that reading would refuse the live org chart. An office that wants the refusal sets this to 1.
# MOLE_OFFICE_REFUSE_MESSAGE_TO_AGENT_WITH_NO_SESSION_AND_NO_RUN=1

# THE DEAD-LETTER BROADCAST TRIGGER. A message to an agent that turned out to be unreachable is kept
# as a dead letter; once the oldest unclaimed one has sat this many days, or this many have piled up,
# the office sends the summary to the named secretary. Unset means one day and more than twenty letters.
# Both rules apply from 3 October 2026, in place of an earlier example, and a person's letter
# to a retired agent goes on at once. src/config/settings.mjs names the decision.
# MOLE_OFFICE_DEAD_LETTER_AFTER_DAYS=1
# MOLE_OFFICE_DEAD_LETTER_AFTER_COUNT=20

# A NAME FOR THIS WHOLE INSTALLATION OF MOLE OFFICE. One installation is one person's Mole Office on
# one machine: the canonical stack, every worktree of it, and every world any of them starts. Every
# world the office lists says which installation it belongs to, so that two offices could be told
# apart if they ever have to talk to each other.
#
# WRITTEN INTO THE DATABASE ONCE and never rewritten, the first time that database is migrated.
# Changing this line afterwards changes nothing about a database that already has its id: what is
# stored is what the citizens of that world belong to, and rewriting it would move them somewhere
# they were never registered. It is read only when the row is not there yet.
#
# Unset means the id is worked out from the path of the DATABASE BEING MIGRATED, hashed the same way
# a world's identity is — not from the path this checkout declares. Migrations are run from a
# worktree before a branch is merged, and a worktree's declaration names itself, so reading the
# declaration stamped a worktree's id into the live database once already.
# MOLE_OFFICE_INSTALLATION=my-computer

# The run's command has three minutes to finish before the office reports open resources.
# MOLE_OFFICE_ENDED_RUN_LOOK_AFTER_SECONDS=180
# Check for due looks twice a minute; sweep all ended runs every five minutes.
# MOLE_OFFICE_ENDED_RUN_LOOK_EVERY_SECONDS=30
# MOLE_OFFICE_ENDED_RUN_SWEEP_EVERY_SECONDS=300

# Look once a minute for messages the person put off with Remind me later whose time has come.
# MOLE_OFFICE_REMIND_ME_LATER_EVERY_SECONDS=60

# How often, in milliseconds, the server looks again at everybody's presence while a person has a page open. Unset, it
# keeps the default in src/shared/presence-clock.ts, as the live office does. A test office sets a few seconds, so a
# presence change shows without a minute's wait. A value the server cannot use stops its start and names this setting.
# MOLE_OFFICE_LOOK_AT_PRESENCE_AGAIN_EVERY_MS=2000

# How often, in milliseconds, the server runs its sleep clock. Unset, it keeps the default in src/shared/sleep-clock.ts,
# once a minute, as the live office does. A test office sets a second, so a sleep whose minute opens is begun within a
# second. A value the server cannot use stops its start and names this setting.
# MOLE_OFFICE_RUN_THE_SLEEP_CLOCK_EVERY_MS=1000

# The longest time, in minutes, a notice batch waits before waking its recipient.
# This gives a quiet agent its batch even when no independent work starts.
# MOLE_OFFICE_NOTICE_BATCH_MAX_MINUTES=60

# Whether a dsh run reaches DeepSeek, and an OpenCode run reaches OpenCode Go, through the provider router:
# on, or off. It is off unless this says on. With on, the launcher gives such a run a token of its own in
# place of the key, and the router, a host process, swaps the token for a key from its key slots file.
# It turns OpenCode Go runs between the Go slots. Check views and isolated execs then get no DeepSeek key
# and no OpenCode Go key, so the office does not start while a Casting row in force for the inspector
# uses dsh or OpenCode Go.
# Anything but on or off is refused.
# MOLE_OFFICE_PROVIDER_ROUTER=off
# The most, in US dollars, one run may spend through the provider router. With no cap, no token is issued.
# MOLE_OFFICE_PROVIDER_ROUTER_RUN_CAP=2.5
# The least free space, in gigabytes of 1,000,000,000 bytes, a stable release must leave on the disk that holds
# its work folder, at its lowest point. The release refuses before it starts when its estimate goes under, and
# stops before downtime when a reading during the release goes under. It is 30 unless set.
# MOLE_OFFICE_STABLE_RELEASE_FLOOR_GB=30
